    <h2>3 decisions with outsized payoffs</h2>
    <p>The tools in this section cover decisions most people make once or a few times, but that carry a lot of money over a career: how to pace 401(k) contributions, when to claim Social Security, and what a commute really costs.</p>

    <h3>Don't let front-loading cost you match</h3>
    <p>Many employers match 401(k) contributions paycheck by paycheck. If you contribute a high percentage and reach the 2026 employee limit of $24,500 in the fall, your contributions stop for the rest of the year, and so does the match, unless your plan makes a "true-up" deposit at year-end. The <a href="../../calculators/401k-match.html">401(k) match calculator</a> finds the contribution rate that maxes out on your last paycheck and shows how much match a faster pace would forfeit. Workers 50 and older can contribute $8,000 more in catch-up contributions, and those aged 60 to 63 can contribute $11,250 more.</p>

    <h3>Claiming age changes Social Security for life</h3>
    <p>For anyone born in 1960 or later, full retirement age is 67. Claiming at 62 permanently reduces the monthly benefit to 70% of the full amount; waiting past 67 adds 8% a year until 70, for 124% of the full amount. The monthly check at 70 is about 77% larger than at 62. Whether waiting pays depends on how long you live, which is why the <a href="../../calculators/social-security-claiming-age.html">Social Security claiming age calculator</a> shows the break-even ages and lifetime totals for each claiming age. For married couples, the higher earner's claiming age also sets the survivor benefit, which often argues for that person waiting.</p>

    <h3>A commute is a pay cut you don't see on a pay stub</h3>
    <p>Fuel, parking, wear on the car, meals out, and work clothes come out of after-tax pay, so covering them takes a larger pre-tax raise. The <a href="../../calculators/return-to-office-commute-cost.html">return-to-office calculator</a> adds them up, counts the unpaid hours on the road, and shows the raise that would break even. It's a useful number to have before negotiating a return-to-office policy, a hybrid schedule, or a job offer with a longer drive.</p>

    <h2>Check the official numbers</h2>
    <p>Your Social Security statement at ssa.gov (my Social Security) gives your actual benefit estimate at each age; enter that figure rather than a guess. Your plan's Summary Plan Description explains how your employer's match is calculated and whether it includes a true-up. Contribution limits are set by the IRS each year, and the calculators are updated when they change.</p>
  </div>
